  "question": {
    "text": "It is found that if a neutron suffers an elastic collinear collision with a deuterium at rest, the fractional loss of its energy is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></msub></math>,&nbsp;while for its similar collision with a carbon nucleus at rest, the fractional loss of energy is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></msub></math>. The values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></msub></math> are respectively",
